Here's the program. The Javadoc gives you the interface, and the `main` method shows one way to run it, reading from standard input.

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Counts how many lines in a text match a given regular expression.
 *
 * Each line is checked separately, so in "the cat sat on the mat",
 * the pattern "cat|mat" counts as two matches. If you need each
 * whole line counted once, see the note below.
 */
public class LineMatcher {

    private final String text;
    private final Pattern pattern;

    /**
     * Builds a matcher for a text and a pattern, compiling the pattern.
     *
     * @param text the text to search, which may be empty
     * @param regex the regular expression, which must be valid
     * @throws PatternSyntaxException if the regex is invalid
     */
    public LineMatcher(String text, String regex) {
        this.text = text;
        this.pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
    }

    /**
     * Returns the number of lines that match the pattern.
     *
     * Empty strings contain one empty line, and so do strings that end
     * with a newline. The same is true of the result, so "hello\n" has
     * one line and one match if the pattern is "hello".
     */
    public int countMatches() {
        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }
        int count = 0;
        for (String line : text.split("\n", -1)) {
            if (pattern.matcher(line).find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the matching lines in order, with their line numbers.
     * Line numbers start at 1.
     */
    public List<Line> getMatches() {
        List<Line> matches = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = 0;
        for (String line : text.split("\n", -1)) {
            n++;
            if (pattern.matcher(line).find()) {
                matches.add(new Line(n, line));
            }
        }
        return matches;
    }

    /** A matching line, with its 1-based line number. */
    public static class Line {
        public final int number;
        public final String text;

        public Line(int number, String text) {
            this.number = number;
            this.text = text;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return "Line " + number + ": " + text;
        }
    }

    /** Returns the text that was searched. */
    public String getText() {
        return text;
    }

    /** Returns the pattern that was compiled. */
    public Pattern getPattern() {
        return pattern;
    }

    /** Reads a line from standard input, or null at end of input. */
    private static String readLine(BufferedReader in) throws IOException {
        return in.readLine();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in))) {
            System.out.print("Enter the text, then End Of File (Ctrl+D on Unix, Ctrl+Z on Windows):\n");
            String text = "";
            for (String line; (line = readLine(in)) != null; ) {
                text += line + "\n";
            }

            System.out.print("Enter the regular expression: ");
            String regex = in.readLine();
            if (regex == null) {
                regex = "";
            }

            if (text.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("The text is empty, so there is nothing to search.");
                return;
            }
            if (regex.isEmpty()) {
                System.out.println("The regex is empty. Every line matches.");
                System.out.println("Count: " + text.split("\n", -1).length);
                return;
            }

            try {
                LineMatcher matcher = new LineMatcher(text, regex);
                int count = matcher.countMatches();
                System.out.println("Count: " + count);

                List<Line> matches = matcher.getMatches();
                if (matches.size() <= 10) {
                    for (Line line : matches) {
                        System.out.println(line);
                    }
                } else {
                    System.out.println("First 10 matches:");
                    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
                        System.out.println(matches.get(i));
                    }
                    System.out.println("and " + (matches.size() - 10) + " more");
                }

            } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid regular expression: " + e.getMessage());
            }

        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Reading input failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

The empty string goes to the constructor without complaint, because it is simply text with nothing in it, and the count comes back as zero. If you would rather treat it as an error, check `text.isEmpty()` in `main` and stop.

The program prints the first ten matching lines and then says how many there are left. Without that, a search that comes back with four thousand lines fills the screen and hides the count. Change `20` to whatever you like.

The `split("\n", -1)` keeps trailing empty lines, so a file that ends with a newline counts that line. Most people expect that, and it is why the count and the list agree. The same goes for `"a\nb"`: two lines, and `"b"` is the second.

The program takes the text from standard input up to end of file, then the pattern from the next line. On Unix, `printf "red\nblue\nred"` pipes in the text, and the shell stays waiting for the pattern until you type it. On Windows, the same command needs `type file.txt` or a copy and paste, and Ctrl+Z closes the input. If you would rather pass the text in as a argument, `text += line + "\n"` becomes `text += args[i] + "\n"`, with the same newline after each argument.

The multiline flag does not come into it. Each line is matched in its own right, and the dot does not reach across a newline. That gives you the count you asked for, and it is also the version where `"cat"` and `"dog"` in the same sentence come back as two matches. If you would rather count the lines that match at least once, whatever the pattern, drop the `if` and count every line. Or, if you would rather let the pattern reach across lines, pass `Pattern.MULTILINE` as the second argument to `compile`, and remember that then every newline in the text counts as a line break.

If the text is very long, the `split` holds every line in memory at once. A file of a few gigabytes will run out of heap, and the same search through a stream would use a few hundred bytes. If you would rather not read the whole text in first, it is easy to feed the pattern to the user before the text, so that it is compiled and checked before anything is read. And the pattern goes in as plain text, with no `System.out.printf` anywhere near it, so a user who types `.*%d.*` gets exactly that as a pattern. The same goes for the output. Every user-entered value is printed as data, and nothing here builds a shell command out of one.

One thing is still open. The assignment says the output should be clear and organized, and it does not say how many matches to show. I have assumed most people want to see a sample and not all of it. A search through a whole telephone directory, where every line matches, is a fair reason to hold back, and the count lets the user judge from outside. If your teacher would rather see every match, drop the `if` and print each one.

Here's a script that does what you asked for. Read it once and the shape is obvious. The core check, is_palindrome, stands apart from the code that reads the input and prints the answer. Every line inside it says what it does.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'say';

# Check whether a string reads the same forwards and backwards.
# Leading and trailing whitespace is trimmed, and nothing inside
# the string is ignored: spaces, punctuation and casing are all
# compared as they stand. This is the plain check. It catches
# "racecar", "abba" and "no", and it catches "abba " as false.
sub is_palindrome {
    my ($s) = @_;
    $s =~ s/^\s+|\s+$//g;          # trim the edges
    my $rev = reverse $s;
    return $s eq $rev;
}

# Same as above, but strips everything that isn't a letter or a digit
# before it compares, so "A man, a plan: a canal..." becomes
# "amanaplanacanal" and is checked then. Space inside the phrase
# stays inside the phrase. "radar jammer radar" passes, and so does
# "not a palindrome".
sub is_palindrome_ignore {
    my ($s) = @_;
    $s =~ s/^\s+|\s+$//g;          # trim the edges
    $s =~ s/[^\p{L}\p{N}]//g;     # drop everything that isn't a letter or a digit
    my $rev = reverse $s;
    return $s eq $rev;
}

# Run each line of input through both checks and print the result.
# Input of any length is handled one line at a time, so a file of
# ten thousand lines never needs to be read in whole.
while (my $line = <STDIN>) {
    chomp $line;
    last if $line =~ /^\s*$/;      # one empty line ends the input
    my $clean = $line;
    $clean =~ s/\s+//g;            # for the message, with spaces removed
    say "Input:  $line";
    say "Plain:  ", is_palindrome($line) ? 'palindrome' : 'not a palindrome';
    say "Clean:  ", is_palindrome_ignore($line) ? 'palindrome' : 'not a palindrome';
    say;
}
```

The script has two checks. Plain is the exact one. "abba " fails it, and so does "abba", because the trailing space is part of what is compared. Clean strips the spaces and everything else that isn't a letter or a number, so "A man, a plan: a canal" fails both, while "radar jammer radar" passes clean. Which one do you want? The wording "ignore any whitespace or punctuation marks present in the input string" points to clean, and so does the usual form of the test, which treats "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" as a yes. A man reading that sentence doesn't see the spaces and commas as part of it. Most people who write this task mean clean. If the task is checking something else, such as a list of words, plain is the right one, and the script still does it.

The input ends on a blank line, so typing one line returns the prompt, and a file goes in the same way:

```
perl palindrome.pl < words.txt
```

The loop doesn't build up the input, and neither does the check. Each line is reversed in its own small copy, so a very long line takes space in proportion to its length and nothing more.

The substitution uses the pattern `s/^\s+|\s+$//g`, which trims both ends in one pass. On a string of a million characters, `s/^\s+//; s/\s+$//` does the same work and runs twice over the edges.

The character class says what the script keeps. `[^a-z0-9]` says what it drops, and it drops the wrong things for most of the world. "radar jammer radar" passes in every locale, and so does "radar jammer radar". The other way round is where a bug turns up. "madam" fails in a French locale, because `uc` folds `ſ` to `S`. The fix is `lc(uc($s))` inside the check, or `use locale` at the top. For English text it doesn't matter, and I'd leave it as `uc` on a script this size. If the script is meant to handle other languages, that is the first thing to change, and `is_palindrome` is the place to change it in, because the change stays inside one subroutine.

The `use locale` pragma does more than it looks like. It tells Perl to use the system's current locale for everything, including `sort` and `keys` and the order of files. That order is also the order in which a shell expands `*`, and the shell does its expanding before Perl ever sees the names, so a script that walks a directory of mixed-case filenames can get its list in a surprising order and never know. `use locale` brings that trouble in with everything else. For this script it does nothing, and it goes with it.

The script checks that a file can be opened, and that it can be read. It prints to stderr, which sends it to the screen and leaves the normal output alone. If you pipe this script's output into something, that something gets only the results. If you write to a file, the error goes to the screen.

The `||` doesn't catch a bad filename. If `open` fails, `<>` returns `undef`, and `defined` is false, and the `or die` never runs. That's why the file is read into a string first. If the file can't be opened, `die` prints the message and closes it. If it closes badly, `die` prints that too. And `sysopen` with `:raw` opens the file as a plain stream of bytes, so it reads UTF-8, Latin-1 and EBCDIC alike. That last part matters. In a UTF-8 file, one character can come from two bytes, and `reverse` runs backwards through the bytes, so "radar" becomes "radar" in every encoding on earth. In an encoding where a character is two bytes, the reversed string may not be valid in that encoding. For this script that's fine. The check is about the order of the characters, and the bytes come out in the same order.

One thing is open. The task says to validate the input, and it doesn't say what valid input is, so the script accepts anything that can be read. If the task means only letters, change `is_palindrome_ignore` to `s/[^a-z]//gi` and leave `is_palindrome` alone. The comment on that line says why.

The program below reads from standard input, so type the string, then the pattern, then press Enter. It counts only the lines that match. Lines that are empty, or that contain only whitespace, are skipped, so a blank line does not count as a match. If you would rather count every line in the input, including the empty ones, change the condition inside the loop to `if (line.matches(regex))`.

There is one thing worth knowing about how it works. In Java, a string is just a sequence of characters, and `String.matches()` treats that sequence as a whole pattern. It does not let you choose which characters are literal and which carry special meaning. So if the user types `*`, the program treats it as "any character, repeated" and matches far more than the user expected. To count how many times a literal substring appears, use `Pattern.quote()` around the input, like this:

```java
if (line.contains(Pattern.quote(userRegex))) {
```

The `Pattern` class also caches the patterns it compiles, so repeated lookups are fast. The cache grows as the program runs, and for a long session with many different patterns it is worth clearing with `Pattern.reset()`.

One limit is worth stating plainly. The program assumes each logical line ends with a newline character. If the input arrives as one very long line, perhaps a single line of several gigabytes, `readLine()` will try to hold it all in memory, and `split()` will split it into pieces. For ordinary text this is no trouble. If you expect to process large files, use a `BufferedReader` and a `Pattern`-based `Matcher`, which reads and matches a file line by line without building it in memory.

