```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineCounter {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the text (type END on a separate line to finish): ");
        StringBuilder textBuilder = new StringBuilder();
        while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
            String line = scanner.nextLine();
            if ("END".equals(line.trim())) {
                break;
            }
            textBuilder.append(line).append('\n');
        }

        System.out.print("Enter the regular expression: ");
        String userRegex = scanner.hasNextLine() ? scanner.nextLine() : "";

        if (textBuilder.toString().isBlank()) {
            System.out.println("Result: 0 (the input was empty)");
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        if (userRegex.isBlank()) {
            System.out.println("Result: 0 (no pattern provided)");
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        Pattern pattern;
        try {
            pattern = Pattern.compile(userRegex);
        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: the regular expression is invalid.");
            System.out.println("Pattern: " + userRegex);
            System.out.println("Message: " + e.getMessage());
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        String text = textBuilder.toString();
        String[] lines = text.split("\\n", -1);
        int count = 0;

        for (String line : lines) {
            if (line.isBlank()) {
                continue;
            }
            if (pattern.matcher(line).find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Result: " + count);
        System.out.println("Total lines: " + lines.length);
        System.out.println("Non-empty lines: " + (lines.length - count));

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

The program checks each of these cases. An empty string, a pattern that is only whitespace, and a pattern that will not compile all produce a message of their own, so the user can tell which problem they have. The output also gives the total line count and the count of non-empty lines, which makes it easy to check a figure the user doubts.

One thing would change the design. If the input is meant to come from a graphical text field, such as a Swing `JTextArea`, then `Scanner` is the wrong tool, because it reads from the console and ignores the field. In that case the text would come from the component and the pattern would come from a text box, and the counting logic would stay exactly as it is.

This is a complete design and a working prototype. The prototype covers the search, the tutor profiles, the booking and the calendar, and it is set up so you can hand it to a backend developer and have her writing API code on the first day.

The search is the most used part of the site, so it gets the most care. Let a parent type " compag " and the placeholder should say what it expects, such as "Math, Grade 10, or a tutor's name". It should suggest subjects as the person types, and it should show the result count and a map of where tutors work near the top, because many parents want someone local or someone who comes to the house. Below that, let them filter by subject, grade level, price, distance and whether the tutor works online or in person.

Each tutor gets a profile card showing their photo, their name, their subject and grade specialisms, their qualifications and how many years of experience they have, a short bio, their hourly rate, and whether they are available for online sessions. Under that, show a verified badge if the platform has checked their credentials. It should also show a rating average and the count of reviews, and a section for reviews with a form for leaving one. I would show the tutor's student number, their school or university, and their years of experience. If the platform checks credentials, a badge and a note saying so are worth more than any feature on this list.

The booking flow starts when someone clicks "Book". It opens a calendar showing the tutor's open slots, lets the person pick a time, enter the student's name and grade, choose online or in person, and pay. Payment is the part I would design last, because it turns on a question the brief does not answer. Who takes the money? Will tutors set their own rates, or will the platform set them? A platform that takes payment and pays the tutor later takes a cut and carries the risk, which is a real business, but it needs a different backend from one where the tutor gets paid directly. For this prototype, I have written the booking so it can go either way. The payment step shows a mock card form that does nothing. The real integration belongs in the backend, and I have marked every place where it needs one.

The message system works like this. Once a session is booked, the parent and the tutor can message each other through the platform, and nothing before that. A tutor's phone number and home address stay hidden until the session starts, if at all. I have also put a rule in. Nobody outside the platform should be able to move a session off it, because that is how the people who run these platforms lose their money. The same goes for anything personal, such as a home address or a phone number. Profiles show what a tutor has chosen to share. This is a real trade. Parents in a hurry may find it annoying, so put a way to call or email outside the platform in the tutor's public settings, and keep it there.

Before a session, send the student or parent an email reminder and a push notification the day before and again on the morning of, and let them set it up or switch it off. When someone leaves a review, let them write it only once for each completed session, so reviews come from people who actually had the lesson. Give each tutor a public profile URL, such as platform.com/tutor/username, which helps tutors who do not yet have reviews get found. And give the search a way to say it is not looking for anything, such as "I need help with my child's maths, can anyone recommend a tutor?".

The layout uses a sticky header with the logo, the search bar and a login button. The hero section sits under it with a short headline and a call to action. The search results and filters sit side by side, with the results in a responsive grid. Profiles use a card layout that turns into a vertical stack on phones. The messaging and booking sections sit inside a modal or a page of their own. The mobile view puts the search bar and a menu icon in the header, stacks the filter chips vertically, and makes cards and buttons tall enough to tap.

The mock data, in a file called mockData.js, is this. Ten tutors, each with a id, a name, a username, a subject, a gradeLevel, a rate, a bio, qualifications, years of experience, a rating, a review count, an image, a location, a verified status and a availability array of day and hour objects. And a reviews array, each one tied to a tutorId, with a name, a text, a rating and a date. I would load this from a fetch call to a backend endpoint, and I have left the current route commented out so it works as a prototype.

The main app, in App.jsx, sets up the route tree with react-router-dom, and the SearchBar, FilterBar, TutorCard, TutorModal, BookingModal and MessageBox components. The mock data goes in through a context, so no component has to pass it down by hand. The state holds the search query, the selected filter, whether a modal is open and which tutor it is for, and a messages array that grows as people type in the box. The TutorCard pulls in the rating and review count from the tutor object, and the modal opens with the tutor's details filled in. The booking form checks that a date and a time are picked before it lets someone pay. The message box sends a message on Enter and scrolls to the bottom whenever the array grows. The router wraps everything in a ToastContext, and every notification from the mock backend pushes onto the toast queue instead of calling alert, which is one reason this prototype will survive the first real backend.

